<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William II of England</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1056–<a href="/years/1100">1100</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as William Rufus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William II, called Rufus, was the third son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a> and king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> from 1087. He put down rebellions by barons who favoured his elder brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-curthose">Robert Curthose</a>, and in 1096 took Normandy in pledge from Robert, who was leaving on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a>. He was killed by an arrow while hunting in the New Forest in 1100 and was succeeded by his brother Henry I.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-england.jpg" alt="William II of England" width="320" height="497" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Matthew Paris · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_II_of_England_Historia_Anglorum.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>William the Conqueror left Normandy to his eldest son Robert Curthose and England to <b>William Rufus</b>; Robert’s weak rule ended when his youngest brother, Henry I of England, defeated and imprisoned him in 1106.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Duchy of Normandy · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Three days after <b>William Rufus</b> died in a hunting accident, his brother Henry was crowned at Westminster.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-5">5 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Forces loyal to the English king <b>William Rufus</b> tried and failed to take control of Rouen, capital of his elder brother Robert Curthose, Duke of Normandy.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-3">3 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Normandy went to his eldest son Robert and England to <b>William Rufus</b>, a division that set his heirs against each other.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-9">9 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Normandy, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in New Forest National Park, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.12,-0.44&amp;year=1084" aria-label="Normandy on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4540 450 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of William II of England"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4990 720h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4990 720h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.12,-0.44&amp;year=1084">What was here in 1084? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
